Manage .env files — validation, secret scanning, and format conversion

Usage: evnx [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>

Commands:
  init         Interactive project setup — the template, .env and the .evnx.toml contract
  add          Add environment variables to existing .env files
  remove       Remove variables from the env file, and the template and contract too [aliases: rm]
  validate     Check .env against .env.example, find issues
  scan         Detect secrets that look real (AWS keys, tokens, etc.)
  diff         Compare two env files: keys missing or extra, and values between environments
  convert      Transform `.env` files into multiple output formats
  migrate      Move a .env into a secret manager: print the commands, or upload to GitHub
  sync         Keep .env and .env.example in sync
  align        Give .env files the template's sections, comments and key order, keeping their values
  template     Generate config files from templates
  backup       Create encrypted backup of .env
  restore      Restore from encrypted backup
  auth         Manage your evnx cloud account
  org          Manage organisations — billing and a directory
  vault        Manage encrypted vaults
  cloud        Sync encrypted .env files with evnx cloud.
  platform     Compare or sync .env with a hosting platform's variables (Vercel).
  doctor       Check .env files, Git config, project structure, and security
  spec         Declare what each variable is: required, format, secret
  history      List the copies evnx kept before changing a file: which file, which command, when
  undo         Put a file back as it was before evnx last changed it
  parse        List what evnx reads from a `.env`: line, key and flags, never a value
  run          Run a command with a `.env`'s variables in its environment
  commands     Every command this build has, and which features it was compiled with [aliases: surface]
  completions  Generate shell completions
  update       Show how to upgrade, and optionally whether a newer release exists
  help         Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Options:
      --no-color  Disable colored output
  -q, --quiet     Print nothing on success. Findings, warnings, errors and requested data still print
  -v, --verbose   Enable verbose output
  -h, --help      Print help
  -V, --version   Print version
